    "title": "Nuggets Dominate Shorthanded Jazz in Blowout - Denver Today",
    "text": "Mar. 2, 2026 at 1:55pm Got story updates? Submit your updates here. ›The 37-24 Denver Nuggets easily handled the 18-42 Utah Jazz, winning by over 20 points in a lopsided affair. With the Jazz missing several key players, including Jaren Jackson Jr., Walker Kessler, Lauri Markkanen, Jusuf Nurkic, Vince Williams Jr., and Kevin Love, the Nuggets' offense led by Nikola Jokic had little trouble dismantling Utah's NBA-worst defense. Why it matters The Nuggets continue to assert their dominance in the Western Conference, led by the MVP-caliber play of Nikola Jokic. Meanwhile, the Jazz's injury-plagued roster struggles to compete against the league's elite teams, raising questions about the direction of the franchise. The details Jokic led the Nuggets with another impressive stat line, recording 28 points, 13 rebounds, and 11 assists while shooting an efficient 57% from the field. Denver's offense was firing on all cylinders, connecting on 39.2% of their three-point attempts as a team. The Jazz, on the other hand, had no answer for the Nuggets' attack, allowing a league-worst 30.5 opponent assists per game. The game tipped off at 9 PM EST on Monday, March 2, 2026. The players Nikola JokicThe star center for the Denver Nuggets, averaging 28.8 points, 12.6 rebounds, and 10.5 assists per game this season.Jaren Jackson Jr.One of the key players the Utah Jazz were missing due to injury. Got photos? Submit your photos here. › What they’re saying “Jokic is simply on another level right now. He's striking for 40.1% from three-point territory as well, even if that's not the main part of his game he's concerned about; he just wants you to know that he's a three level scorer.”— Bill Yin, Sports Analyst What’s next The Nuggets will look to continue their strong play as they push for a high playoff seed in the Western Conference. The Jazz, meanwhile, will need to get healthy and find ways to improve their league-worst defense if they hope to be competitive in the second half of the season. The takeaway Nikola Jokic's MVP-caliber performance led the Nuggets to a dominant victory over the shorthanded Jazz, highlighting the stark contrast between a contending team and one rebuilding through injuries. Denver's offensive efficiency and Jokic's all-around brilliance proved too much for Utah to handle.",
